## 4. Login

The student enters:

* Username or student ID
* Password

After successful authentication, the student is taken to the dashboard.

## 5. Dashboard

The dashboard can display:

* Search Knowledge Base
* Create Ticket
* My Tickets
* Ticket Status
* Feedback
* Help/Support

The dashboard should use simple labels and clear navigation.

## 6. Creating a Ticket

The student provides:

* Problem title
* Problem description
* Category
* Priority
* Optional supporting information

After submission, the system generates a unique Ticket ID.

### Example

```text
Ticket ID: T1025
Category: Network
Problem: Wi-Fi is not connecting
Priority: High
Status: Open
```

## 7. Tracking a Ticket

Students should be able to view the current status of their ticket.

```text id="p7g3kg"
Open
  ↓
Assigned
  ↓
In Progress
  ↓
Resolved
  ↓
Closed
```

The student can also view support responses and resolution details.

## 8. Support Staff Interaction

Support staff receive assigned tickets through their dashboard.

They can:

1. Open a ticket
2. Read the problem
3. Check the knowledge base
4. Troubleshoot the problem
5. Send a response
6. Update the ticket status
7. Mark the ticket as resolved

## 9. Administrator Interaction

Administrators manage the overall system.

They can:

* Add or remove users
* Manage support staff
* Monitor tickets
* Update knowledge-base content
* Review feedback
* Generate reports

## 10. Communication Protocol

Communication between students and support staff should be:

* Clear
* Simple
* Professional
* Respectful
* Easy to understand

Technical instructions should be provided step by step.

## 11. Feedback Interaction

After the ticket is resolved, the student receives a feedback option.

The student can provide:

* Rating
* Comments
* Problem resolution status
* Suggestions

The feedback is stored in the database for analysis.

## 12. Error Handling

The system should display clear messages when an error occurs.

### Examples

```text
"Please enter all required information."

"Invalid login details."

"Ticket created successfully."

"Your ticket has been resolved."

"Please try again later."
```

## 13. User-Friendly Design

The system should follow these principles:

* Simple navigation
* Clear buttons
* Easy-to-read text
* Consistent layout
* Helpful error messages
* Quick access to support
* Mobile-friendly design

## 14. Security

User interactions should be protected using:

* Secure login
* User authentication
* Role-based access
* Session management
* Secure data handling

Students should only be able to access their own tickets and information.

## 15. Benefits

A good user interaction protocol provides:

* Easy system usage
* Better communication
* Faster problem reporting
* Easy ticket tracking
* Improved user satisfaction
* Reduced confusion

## 16. Conclusion

The User Interaction Protocol ensures that students, support staff, and administrators can use the system effectively. A simple and well-organized interface can improve the overall support experience and make the system suitable for an academic environment.
